{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788197097,"nonce":"Ce7OA+3PbJ8Yr4UwKzRhd669MVM=","message":"PRE-MERGE AUDIT of `spec/manual-work-priorities` (read-only worker, never launched\nthe bench). Verdict: **merge after the one fix I committed** — `d31ed19`, on the\nbranch, source only, clean `-c Release` 0 warnings.\n\n\nChecked by member name against `misc/rimworld/reference/decompiled/RimWorldBase`:\n\n- `RimWorld/MainTabWindow_Work.cs` `DoManualPrioritiesCheckbox` — the checkbox is\n  drawn at `new Rect(5f, 5f, 140f, 30f)` of the same `MainTabWindow` whose body is\n  the priority matrix, it is UNCONDITIONAL (no gate), and on change it runs\n  `foreach (Pawn item in PawnsFinder.AllMapsWorldAndTemporary_Alive) if (item.Faction\n  == Faction.OfPlayer \u0026\u0026 item.workSettings != null) item.workSettings\n  .Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged();`. The branch's reproduction is clause-for-clause.\n- `RimWorld/Pawn_WorkSettings.cs` `Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged` is\n  `workGiversDirty = true;` and nothing else, and it does NOT call\n  `ConfirmInitializedDebug` — so gating on `workSettings != null` rather than\n  `EverWork` is correct, and the fan-out cannot fire the Class B red error.\n- `WorkGiversInOrderNormal` / `WorkGiversInOrderEmergency` call\n  `CacheWorkGiversInOrder()` only when `workGiversDirty`. **The inert-flip claim is\n  exactly right, and stronger than the issue states:** grepping\n  `Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged` over the whole 1.6 tree returns TWO hits — the\n  declaration and that one call site. Nothing else in the game ever dirties the cache\n  for this reason; only an unrelated `SetPriority` / `DisableAll` would.\n- `GetPriority` is verbatim as quoted, `Humanlike` gate included; `SetPriority` writes\n  raw and sets `workGiversDirty`; `ExposeData` scribes the raw `DefMap`. **Lossless is\n  correct.** `WidgetsWork.DrawWorkBoxFor`'s else-branch and\n  `PawnColumnWorker_WorkPriority.HeaderClicked`'s else-branch write only 0 and 3 —\n  the \"a write while off flattens a stored 1/2/4\" warning is correct.\n- `RimWorld/PlaySettings.cs` — `public bool useWorkPriorities;` (no initialiser) and\n  `Scribe_Values.Look(ref useWorkPriorities, \"useWorkPriorities\", defaultValue: false)`.\n  Default-OFF confirmed. `useWorkPriorities` has exactly ONE write site in the tree\n  (the checkbox) and four read sites, none of which mutate.\n- `Find.PlaySettings =\u003e Current.Game.playSettings` (`Verse/Find.cs`), so the setter and\n  `PawnSerializer.WorkRow`'s `use_priorities` read the same field. Read/write agree.\n- `MainButtonWorker.Disabled` is `Find.CurrentMap == null \u0026\u0026 (!def.validWithoutMap ||\n  def == MainButtonDefOf.World)`; the header abbreviates the parenthesis but is right\n  for the Work tab, which `Map()` already reproduces.\n- Class A / R clean: `PawnsFinder.AllMapsWorldAndTemporary_Alive`,\n  `PawnsFinder.AllMaps` and `WorldPawns.AllPawnsAlive` are all\n  `\u003cstatic list\u003e.Clear(); AddRange(…); return \u003cstatic list\u003e` — non-scribed buffers, no\n  lazy init, no `Rand` draw. The snapshot-first departure is justified and correct.\n\nFaithful-to-the-widget note, no action: vanilla's fan-out set omits\n`AllCaravansAndTravellingTransporters_Alive`, so a colonist away in a caravan when the\nbox is ticked keeps a stale WorkGiver order. That is vanilla's bug, reproduced exactly;\ncaravans are a v1 non-goal.\n\n\n`Outcome.Result` (`Source/AutoRimmer/PawnActs.cs:247`) derives the provenance stamp from\n`Accepted.Count \u003e 0 ? Stamp(seq) : NoStamp()`. **The matrix path of `work-priorities`\nnever calls `Outcome.Ok`** — its unit is matrix CELLS, so it fills `changes` and then\noverwrites `result[\"accepted\"]` and `result[\"counts\"]` afterwards. `Accepted.Count` was\ntherefore always 0, and every successful matrix write returned\n\n    \"action\": {\"journal_seq\": null, \"provenance\": \"not applicable — nothing was mutated\"}\n\nover a call that had just written journal line `seq` via `Act(V, \"set\", …)`. The journal\nrow was real; the result denied it.\n\nThat is a false negative on the join key the provenance model rests on, and 3.4 asserts\nagainst it directly — **check 4.7e, `Ge 'data.action.journal_seq' 1`**. 4.7e stood green\nonly because 4.7 was one of the eight checks THIS issue makes reachable: it was being\nrefused with `ok:false` and never reached the assertion. So the branch's own purpose\nwould have failed on its first bench run, one check short.\n\nFixed at `PawnManageVerbs.cs:338` by stamping from the counter the path actually keeps,\nwith `ManualFallbackStamp()` (`:347`) for the combined form — a call whose matrix or copy\nhalf wrote nothing but whose `manual` flip landed HAS mutated and does have a journal\nline, so `NoStamp()` would be untrue there too. The copy path gets the same treatment at\n`:231`. `accept/manual-work-priorities.md` step 4 now asserts the `journal_seq` clause.\n\n\n**An `ok:false` from `work-priorities` does not imply the colony is unchanged.** The flip\nruns at `PawnManageVerbs.cs:153`, before `OnePawn` / `PawnList` / `WorkTypeList` / the\npriority-range check — all of which throw `VerbArgsException`. So\n`{manual:true, set:[{pawn:A, work:\"Docter\", priority:1}]}` returns bad-args with the\nlever already flipped, fanned out and journaled. That ordering is not an accident: it is\nwhat makes the one-call form work and it is why the refusal re-evaluates correctly. The\ncost is real and was undocumented, so it is now stated in the header comment above the\nflip and given an acceptance step (16 in `accept/manual-work-priorities.md`) rather than\nleft to be discovered. **If the verb should be atomic instead, the fix is to resolve every\n`set` block before the flip and re-check the refusal after it — a restructure of the\nmatrix loop, not a line, and I did not do it unilaterally.**\n\nAlso noted, not acted on: `NotifyUseWorkPrioritiesChanged` uses `Faction.OfPlayer`\n(`RimWorld/Faction.cs`), which `Log.Error`s \"Could not find player faction\" when null.\n`Faction.OfPlayerSilentFail` would be strictly safer under the zero-red-errors invariant.\nUnreachable in practice — `Map()` succeeded first — and vanilla's widget uses the loud\nform too, so it stays as reproduced.\n\n\nLever ORDER is right (`:153-155`, `usePriorities` is reassigned from `manual[\"after\"]`\nbefore the refusal loop reads it) — not the `39c9db7` warden shape. Idempotence is right:\nre-issuing writes the same value, `changed:false`, `pawns_notified:0`, `NoStamp()`, no\njournal line, no double-notify, matching the widget's own `if (changed)` guard.\n`VerbArgs.Bool` throws on a non-bool, so `{\"manual\":\"true\"}` is bad-args, not a silent\nno-op. The read side agrees. `copy_from` correctly takes its clipboard AFTER the flip.\n\n\nAgainst current `origin/main` (`dba8813`) this branch conflicts only on **DESIGN.md**,\nand all three of main / this branch / `spec/stable-pawn-order` are PURE APPENDS at the\ntail of the decisions log (+85 / +62 / +56, zero deletions). Keep all three entries.\n\nAgainst `spec/stable-pawn-order`: `.py` and `.ps1` auto-merge and — checked, not assumed —\nthe auto-merge is SEMANTICALLY correct. In the `.py`, step 0.5 lands inside\n`phase0_journal()`, the function stable-pawn-order extracted, so it runs on both the\ndry-run and live paths. `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.md` conflicts in ONE hunk (adjacent\nfixture-table rows), additive, resolution is keep-both. The envelope/check counts in that\nfile's header need reconciling by hand: both branches changed the run and neither number\ncovers the other's change (76 fixed envelopes + one `pawn` per colonist, 140 checks).\n\nStill owes a `Build:` commit at merge; the DLL is deliberately uncommitted.","files":null}]}